<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frankfurt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frankfurt am Main is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> it was a wealthy commercial centre and the traditional place where the emperors were elected, and later also crowned. It remains a major financial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frankfurt.jpg" alt="Frankfurt" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dr. Thomas Liptak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frankfurt_am_Main,_Central_business_district_(2024).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…from 1821, made Austria the guardian of the settlement of 1815: he hosted the Congress of Vienna, presided over the German Confederation, in which Austria held the permanent chair of the Federal Diet at <b>Frankfurt</b>, and pushed through the Carlsbad Decrees of 1819 against liberal and national agitation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Avars are last mentioned in 822, when their envoys appeared at an assembly of Louis the Pious at <b>Frankfurt</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>A founding member of the European Economic Community, it signed the Maastricht Treaty in 1992 and the Lisbon Treaty in 2007 and co-founded the eurozone; the European Central Bank sits in <b>Frankfurt</b>, its largest financial centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The name of the Franks survives in France, in the German region of Franconia and in the city of <b>Frankfurt</b>, the ‘ford of the Franks’.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Congress of Vienna, joined 39 sovereign states and free cities: the Austrian Empire and the Kingdom of Prussia for their former imperial lands, kingdoms such as Bavaria, Saxony, Württemberg and Hanover, dozens of grand duchies, duchies and principalities, and the free cities of <b>Frankfurt</b>, Hamburg, Bremen and Lübeck.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The state, officially named the Grand Duchy of Hesse and by Rhine, was thus composed of three provinces: Starkenburg around Darmstadt, Upper Hesse around Giessen, separated from the rest by a strip of <b>Frankfurt</b> and Hesse-Kassel territory, and Rhenish Hesse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
